      "sample_index": 2,
      "text": "To determine the probability that both the two-digit number \\(ab\\) and the individual digits \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are all divisible by 3 when two fair 6-sided dice are tossed, we proceed as follows:\n\nFirst, identify the possible values for \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) such that each is divisible by 3. The numbers on a fair 6-sided die are \\(1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6\\). The numbers divisible by 3 in this range are \\(3\\) and \\(6\\).\n\nThis means there are 2 choices for \\(a\\) and 2 choices for \\(b\\), so there are a total of \\(2 \\times 2 = 4\\) possible outcomes where both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3.\n\nNext, calculate the total number of possible outcomes when two dice are tossed. Since each die has 6 faces, the total number of outcomes is \\(6 \\times 6 = 36\\).\n\nThe probability that both \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are divisible by 3 is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes. Therefore, the probability is:\n\n\\[\n\\frac{4}{36} = \\frac{1}{9}\n\\]\n\nThus, the probability that both the two-digit number \\(ab\\) and the individual digits \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) are all divisible by 3 is \\(\\boxed{\\frac{1}{9}}\\).",
